    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d343">

                <pb facs="0485" n="395" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg395"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00 R 29/254: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wallace</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-07-01T12:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 1,
                                1920—noon</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received 11:58 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>1344. B–137 for Davis.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1st.</label>
                    <item>Keneipp tells me that discussion in which proportions of any indemnity
                        received by France and England, were fixed at, France 55 parts, England 25
                        parts, was based on report of some subcommittee largely if not wholly
                        American, which contained estimates of damages of various countries,
                        estimates being fairly definite for England, France, Belgium, rather hazy
                        for Italy. This paper then in possession Loucheur,<anchor n="72"
                            xml:id="d343-f1"/> but neither Bradbury nor myself able to find any
                        copy. I have been searching our files here, also Embassy files without
                        success. Can you give me any indication where this document can now be
                        found, or where reference is made to it in any of the <hi
                            rendition="#italic">procès</hi> [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >verbaux</hi>]?</item>
                    <label>2d.</label>
                    <item>I get the impression that Lamont<anchor n="73" xml:id="d343-f2"/> had
                        something to do with the matter, so consult him if you have not required
                        information. In any case please cable your or his best recollection of
                        figures. Some approximation would be helpful to Keneipp if nothing better
                        can be obtained. This document has bearing on pending discussions. You will
                        recognize importance of haste. Boyden.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wallace</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d343-f1" n="72" xml:id="d343-f1-content">
                <p>L. Loucheur, French representative on the Organization Committee of the
                    Reparation Commission, and chairman of the committee.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d343-f2" n="73" xml:id="d343-f2-content">
                <p>Thorns W. Lamont, technical adviser, American Commission to Negotiate Peace.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
